#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209870} :activity/id #uuid "e03cea40-d542-50a2-beef-0257307c0a4f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050484 :topic/id #uuid "4d4dd9cc-d32a-5514-bbc8-b9d4357f3376" :topic/math-academy-id 6007} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209878 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll extend our knowledge of gradients of scalar functions to include quadratic functions, quadratic forms, and the chain rule.\n\nConsider the (scalar) **quadratic function**\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$ is a matrix, and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ is a column vector. We wish to calculate the gradient of this function:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf x} f = \\nabla_{\\mathbf x} \\left(\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe start by showing that $f$ can be written in the expanded form \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j.\n$$\n\nTo understand why this is the same as $\\mathbf x^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf x,$ let's first write\n![](images/39/394b4241f76616fae493bcc281cbe782c1d1a89680ef24cd2776c02302b9550d.svg)\n\nNow, we compute $\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}$ by working step by step.\n\nFirst, we compute the product $\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}$, which gives us an $n\\times 1$ column vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & ⋯ & {a}_{1n} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & ⋯ & {a}_{2n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ {a}_{n1} & {a}_{n2} & ⋯ & {a}_{nn}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}{x}_{1}+{a}_{12}{x}_{2}+⋯+{a}_{1n}{x}_{n} \\\\ {a}_{21}{x}_{1}+{a}_{22}{x}_{2}+⋯+{a}_{2n}{x}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {a}_{n1}{x}_{1}+{a}_{n2}{x}_{2}+⋯+{a}_{nn}{x}_{n}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{1j}{x}_{j} \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{2j}{x}_{j} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{nj}{x}_{j}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply $\\mathbf{x}^T$ by this result:\n![](images/15/15180b9898708fa3f2398a534c0e4f0a57f4466abd046b388eb7af3009e534b0.svg)\n\nEach term of this product looks like $x_i \\cdot \\displaystyle\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_j$, so the full expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} = \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\left( \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_j \\right) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j.\n$$\n\nSo, we've shown that\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j.}\n$$\n\nLet's now discuss how this helps us to compute the gradient of $f.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "46d7287c-3842-57d9-8f8b-8f5f95c5362f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12386 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b43433a0-9dff-53f0-b8cf-64336b4b2fc7" :step/math-academy-id 610963 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209871}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209871 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209954 :tutorial/content "We have the quadratic function\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$ is a matrix, and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ is a column vector. As we've seen, $f$ can be written in the expanded form\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n A_{ij} x_i x_j.\n$$\n\nWe'll use this form to compute the gradient of $f.$\n\nTo compute the gradient $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f$, we'll differentiate this expanded form with respect to $x_k.$ First, we express our expanded function as\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) =  \\sum_{i \\neq k} \\sum_{j \\neq k} A_{ij} x_i x_j \n+ \\sum_{i \\neq k} A_{ik} x_i x_k \n+ \\sum_{j \\neq k} A_{kj} x_k x_j \n+ A_{kk} x_k^2.\n$$\n\nThe idea here is to break up the double summation by isolating all the terms that involve the variable $x_k$. \n\nNotice that\n\n- the first summation is *independent* of $x_k,$\n\n- the second and third summations are *linear* in $x_k,$ and\n\n- the final term is *quadratic* in $x_k.$\n\nWe can now compute our derivative term by term in a structured way. Differentiating with respect to $x_k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{k}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{k}}[{\\sum }_{i\\ne k}{\\sum }_{j\\ne k}{A}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}+{\\sum }_{i\\ne k}{A}_{ik}{x}_{i}{x}_{k}+{\\sum }_{j\\ne k}{A}_{kj}{x}_{k}{x}_{j}+{A}_{kk}{x}_{k}^{2}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i\\ne k}{A}_{ik}{x}_{i}+{\\sum }_{j\\ne k}{A}_{kj}{x}_{j}+2{A}_{kk}{x}_{k} \\\\  & =({\\sum }_{i\\ne k}{A}_{ik}{x}_{i})+{A}_{kk}{x}_{k}+({\\sum }_{j\\ne k}{A}_{kj}{x}_{j})+{A}_{kk}{x}_{k} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{A}_{ik}{x}_{i}+{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{A}_{kj}{x}_{j} \\\\  & =({A}^{T}x)_{k}+(Ax)_{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is true for all $1\\leq k \\leq n$, we get the result\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}) = A^T \\mathbf{x} + A \\mathbf{x} = (A^T + A)\\mathbf x.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we have the following special cases:\n\n- If $A$ is symmetric, then $A^T = A,$ $f(\\mathbf x)$ is a quadratic form, and our result simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}) = 2 A \\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\n- Suppose that $A = I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix. Then, our result for symmetric matrices simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T  \\mathbf{x}) = 2 \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhich is a result we saw in a previous lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "332e4bab-6a1d-5868-a616-4a2c034c2619" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13115 :tutorial/title "The Gradient of a Quadratic Function"} :step/id #uuid "b7685ad5-7aa7-532e-936b-bd0ad89e40bd" :step/math-academy-id 610964 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209872}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209872 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208271 :question/id #uuid "a06524d0-6980-52eb-b5fb-2ca7d0c1dbac" :question/math-academy-id "e-17350"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fe0d364-ef22-5138-9ee5-e473bd909447" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049450}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540069}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Gradient of a Quadratic Form"} :step/id #uuid "1f3213b1-5ea3-5cd6-9b86-84bd1ef095b3" :step/math-academy-id 610965 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209873}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209873 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208289 :question/id #uuid "b4e84c40-15d1-585f-84c7-c615979cd8f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-18713"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2198cf08-3a99-5e5d-80af-bbe722b51647" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540068}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Gradient of a Quadratic Function"} :step/id #uuid "bc662f29-b540-527e-bb28-752fc9820d44" :step/math-academy-id 610968 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209874}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209874 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209877 :tutorial/content "Now, let $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^q$ and $f: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}$. Suppose we want to compute the gradient \n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x})).\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to apply the chain rule. The chain rule for total derivatives tells us that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{1 \\times n}\n= \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{1 \\times q}\n\\cdot \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the gradient of a scalar function is the transpose of the total derivative:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f}_{n \\times 1} = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n$$\n\nTo derive the chain rule in terms of gradients, we apply the identity $(AB)^T = B^T A^T$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})}^{T} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T} \\\\  & ={{{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}}_{⏟}}_{n\\times q}\\cdot {{{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}}_{⏟}}_{q\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient chain rule is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n\\cdot \n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} f.\n$$\n\nNote that the order matters; when expressed in the gradient form, the derivatives are applied *from right to left!*" :tutorial/id #uuid "eb90df7d-906c-5d50-a61a-40222511f26e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12301 :tutorial/title "Computing Gradients With the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "42b51395-66f8-5f41-b3b6-f3e548175320" :step/math-academy-id 610971 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209875}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209875 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208272 :question/id #uuid "fce0099e-b010-50a4-bf0d-51aca4380129" :question/math-academy-id "e-17865"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c77271f-3d21-5193-a1ad-5c0264e337f0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540048}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule to Gradients"} :step/id #uuid "2a7727ac-4542-5d23-af4c-7a22c6bc3047" :step/math-academy-id 610972}] :activity/title "Further Vector Gradients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186208872}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540159} :activity/id #uuid "abde7dfb-d488-5e8b-96d4-bfad19b4670d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050484 :topic/id #uuid "4d4dd9cc-d32a-5514-bbc8-b9d4357f3376" :topic/math-academy-id 6007} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540132 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540057 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45292744-8b9c-5c5e-b27f-372a2cae9e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539608}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539608} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f8172f4-c9fe-5e8d-b6a6-b70f7a350883" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539959} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85a4f0d1-a936-5ab3-bd96-2206322e6e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539699}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539699} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4950606-28b0-554b-95e7-9a39d66b5c16" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6d939167-bbdf-5642-a79d-3234f69c9c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-276043" :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+2 \\\\ 0-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3eaf924d-cd34-5820-a14e-44d3eca661aa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540134}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abd7469e-6019-5f0b-8db1-f38b3d8887b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539770}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539770} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1453ef1-7ffc-594f-a954-83c62ec2f697" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbd51744-3817-5b56-83ea-b6c1a15c1145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539904}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539904} :answer-field/id #uuid "e69fbcb6-2b3e-5e04-95d1-2af8c246cb30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8b961be3-a61d-5fd5-a33f-daa3b859082d" :question/math-academy-id "q-276044" :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 0-10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "44fdf552-67f4-506b-8352-bf3ff777ad37"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0288d1c8-90da-55c4-98e8-5a5eabefe8cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539481} {:answer/id #uuid "86d96929-a573-5ba1-a568-15337e2bcf56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -29\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539703} {:answer/id #uuid "cc83261d-2254-5227-8497-098b5fbcaed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -29\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539828} {:answer/id #uuid "e136544d-ec3c-563b-b13e-653feae14454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539864} {:answer/id #uuid "ff114911-6bea-5d83-a4a0-4820ac877a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539914}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539828} :answer-field/id #uuid "6839df18-40b0-55d8-b0ee-244e7c3bdebb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8b2d453b-0edb-52e1-a8ad-599845d2374a" :question/math-academy-id "q-269515" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y)={y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -29\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6c50ec86-f31c-5ab7-bdc2-7da6231b1be3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540161}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110945c0-e65b-5532-b050-4bd230c8fb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539510} {:answer/id #uuid "1e5de0f5-f94e-561a-ac4c-228d4c9f8358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539539} {:answer/id #uuid "70ee273e-1adf-5b0b-9c25-84a6f752229b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539669} {:answer/id #uuid "778742bb-372b-5ab3-9144-d2501865782a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539679} {:answer/id #uuid "a4929fc1-3e70-5bff-a80f-2be44c2ad920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539756} :answer-field/id #uuid "81f5b530-9e0f-51b8-82ba-e6d74b6b4686" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8ea8eb91-918f-5aeb-b452-a90611b4dd2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-265760" :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Writing $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "99b9d747-8358-5805-8669-518304d418d4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540149}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540161 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263e9c79-2b5d-5fc6-ae10-bec804651dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 42\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539553} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6bef22-fee1-53f1-8d0f-2529ad295f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}30 & 42\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539639} {:answer/id #uuid "85ee42d0-6b46-5068-8546-0b6d45122f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539700} {:answer/id #uuid "a2df55cc-4d93-55a0-9838-4a5a0b419174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}58 & 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539752} {:answer/id #uuid "ae374fd7-8d71-5715-bb99-e0fa0ee5417f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539776}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539776} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4ae1559-7267-51e9-bbdf-d599082e8f60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f634cb0-d91f-55a0-8036-9bf4e6795f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-269525" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-2b)^{T}(Ax-2b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-2b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-2b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-2b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ac05d334-16c5-5e79-9fe0-8447dbb842a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540132}}] :activity/title "Further Vector Gradients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539475}]}
